00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.160
Well, the first theme
I want to talk about

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.950
is richness of functionality.

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.940
BigQuery, when it first
started, was a SQL engine

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.940
that was just there
to analyze data

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:29.470
that you load into the query.

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:31.240
But over the years,
we've added more

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.780
and more functionality
to simplify

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:36.400
how you manage your
data and give you

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.840
more functionality around that.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.240
So richness of
functionality is the theme

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.440
I'm talking about here.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.510
The first one is DDL,
Data Definition Language.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.630
DDL went beta a few months ago.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:54.550
DDL essentially allows you
to inline in SQL statements,

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:58.690
allows you to create and
delete tables and views,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:01.180
virtual views.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.810
So it just makes it much easier
for you to work with your data.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:08.960
You can always do that using
other types of operations.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.300
One interesting way of working
with Data Definition Language

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.800
is using Create Table as select.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.050
So one use case
here for example is,

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:24.460
if you have a table that
isn't partitioned and maybe

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.160
doesn't have a specific
column that you'd like,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.780
you can select from that
table and create new columns.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:37.067
You can filter data or actually
select from any query you want.

00:07:37.067 --> 00:07:38.650
You can have
[? joins. ?] You can have

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.936
analytic functions in there.

00:07:40.936 --> 00:07:42.310
And the results
of that table can

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.550
be written into a new table
that may or may not exist.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:47.565
And then, you can add a
partition there on top.

00:07:47.565 --> 00:07:49.750
So this is probably
the number one use

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:54.160
case for Data Definition
Language that we have so far.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:55.240
So please try out.

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.490
We'd love to hear
your feedback on that.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.530
The next feature is Merge.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:07.720
So BigQuery has had DML,
Data Manipulation Language,

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.660
for quite some time now.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.040
Merge is a really
powerful operation

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:19.360
that merges the three statements
that are a part of DML--

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.380
like insert, update, and delete.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.610
So in one statement merge,
you can do all the above.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:31.810
So this is an example of
a merge query right here.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.730
And the typical use
case is that you

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:37.780
might have a table that
has all your inventory.

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:40.870
And this, table of course,
needs to be kept up to date.

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:44.860
As new inventory comes
in, you would probably

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:49.970
want to update your inventory
table with the new arrivals.

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.350
And of course, if
there is a product

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.940
that's in your
inventory, you might

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.620
want to increase the quantity.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:58.900
And if there is not a
product in your inventory,

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:00.380
you might just want to add it.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.230
So this is what this
statement accomplishes.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:07.870
And a quick little segue,
BigQuery and technologies

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:11.930
like BigQuery that support DML--

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:16.340
support mutations, updates,
deletes, and inserts--

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.370
so BigQuery isn't necessarily
a great technology

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:25.450
where you would want to do
a million individual updates

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:26.330
and deletes.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.694
That's just not how
this technology works.

00:09:28.694 --> 00:09:30.610
And any technology that
is similar to BigQuery

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:34.360
has the same
architectural limitations.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.590
But what BigQuery
allows you to do,

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:40.270
it allows you to
work on as many rows

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:43.900
in a table as possible within
one individual statement.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:46.720
So while we don't offer you
a whole lot of statements

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:48.700
that you want to
make, each statement

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.880
can process essentially trillion
rows if you'd like it to be.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:56.530
We allow you to run
correlated mutations.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:00.880
What I mean by that is, you can
update, delete, and insert rows

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:04.060
in your table based on
some kind of statement

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.200
like a SQL statement.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.110
So here is an example there.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.810
That SQL statement can
be any kind of statement.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:11.380
It can be joins.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.600
You can have aggregations,
filters, analytics,

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.210
and all that typical SQL stuff.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:23.080
So again, while you could do
lots and lots of statements

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:27.070
on mutability, you
probably shouldn't.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.590
And so the best
practice around BigQuery

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:34.930
is to batch all of your updates,
deletes into single states.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:38.320
This is, of course, especially
relevant for things like GDPR,

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:42.520
where you might want to update
your data inside of BigQuery.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.720
So we try to make
it easy on you.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:51.995
The next feature is actually
a data type, numeric.

00:10:51.995 --> 00:10:57.100
A numeric is really
a decimal data type

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.610
that has actual
precision-- so all the way

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:03.940
up to 38 precise decimal points.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:09.020
This is especially useful
for financial calculations

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.160
where you're keeping track
of money essentially.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:15.580
It turns out that float is
not a really good data type

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:17.320
for these types of operations.

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:20.200
People don't like
losing parts of pennies

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:21.220
in their calculations.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:25.210
And neither is integer
because cents exist.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.936
But with this data
type, of course,

00:11:26.936 --> 00:11:28.685
we extended all of our
SQL functionalities

00:11:28.685 --> 00:11:31.420
so you can run
algebraic and arithmetic

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.840
statements in SQL or numeric.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.340
So hopefully, that
simplifies your life.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:41.000
And let us know how
it works for you.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.200
So we talked about the
features that enrich

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:48.440
the functionality of BigQuery.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.200
The next thing I
want to talk about

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.640
are features that
simplify your life, that

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.590
allow you to have
a peace of mind

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:58.660
when you're using BigQuery.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.290
The first one this CMEK.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:05.390
CMEK stands for Customer-Managed
Encryption Keys.

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:12.940
So to take a step back, BigQuery
by default encrypts all data.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:16.780
At rest and what
BigQuery actually does

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:19.960
is, it chunks all your data
into tiny little pieces

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.860
throughout its data centers.

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:25.330
And these little
chunks of data, they're

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:29.830
all protected by their
own data encryption keys.

00:12:29.830 --> 00:12:32.380
And on top of that, we
have key encryption keys

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:35.730
that are encrypting the keys
that are protecting the data,

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:40.300
so that in some unfortunate
situation, where

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:44.380
maybe a disc ends up
in somebody's hands,

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.690
that data is entirely
useless to those folks--

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:50.380
because not only do we chunk
the data all around our data

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:53.560
centers, we also use two
levels of encryption.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:57.770
But we are the ones that are
managing your encryption keys.

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:00.220
So for some situations,
our customers

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.590
might want to manage
their own encryption keys.

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.870
So you bring your
own encryption keys.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:05.780
You manage them.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:06.750
You can rotate them.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.400
You can specify these
encryption keys.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:10.890
You can actually,
with one click,

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.670
de-activate the
encryption keys, so

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.450
that all data
becomes essentially

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:17.650
inaccessible to
anyone, including us.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:20.080
So it's a really interesting
and really powerful

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.390
security feature for our folks.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:25.540
And finally, we're keeping
track of everything

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:28.660
that's happening with
encryption keys and audit logs.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:31.630
This is our paper trail.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:35.470
You should probably go and
enable all Audit Logs today.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:40.450
Audit logs keep track of every
single job, every single query

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:43.610
that has occurred
inside of BigQuery.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.840
So when a job started, when
somebody accessed specific data

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:49.750
set, these are of
course immutable.

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:51.610
And you could
actually pop them back

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.950
into BigQuery for analysis.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:03.130
For the last few years, BigQuery
has been a regional service.

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:07.430
But we've only been inside of
the United States and Europe.

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:10.810
But we have lots and lots
of customers in Asia.

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.960
The primary reason
for folks in Asia

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:19.360
to actually have
BigQuery in the APAC area

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.500
is to minimize transit from
those areas to the United

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:27.760
States or to Europe
because data transfer can

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:30.040
have an additional cost.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.380
Latency-wise, it's not
a huge consideration

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:35.710
because BigQuery takes a
second to process a query.

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:38.590
What's another 100
milliseconds to move that data

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:41.357
from the United States to Tokyo?

00:14:41.357 --> 00:14:42.940
But the folks have
been asking for it.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.940
And so we have announced the
rollout of BigQuery General

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.490
Availability in Tokyo.

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:52.450
So it covers the region.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.660
So you don't have to
go across the ocean.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:58.300
And it allows you to keep
your data in one region.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:01.000
Of course, there
are use cases where

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.970
data sovereignty is applicable.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.580
And so we're going to continue
to look into other areas

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:09.880
where that's applicable.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.590
We've rolled out
several features

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:15.010
around transparency of BigQuery.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:19.780
The first one is, if you are
using the BigQuery UX today,

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:24.340
you can, in real time, see
progress of your query.

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.094
You may have noticed
that already, right?

00:15:26.094 --> 00:15:28.510
It will say in there-- when
you run a query-- it will say,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:32.140
your query is on stage 5 of 12.

00:15:32.140 --> 00:15:34.150
And it will actually show
you a chart of what's

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:36.700
happening inside of that query.

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:42.910
The second is BigQuery
slots is a unit of compute.

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:47.500
That's how BigQuery
executes its workloads.

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:50.440
You can actually
see how many slots

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:52.360
have been consumed
over the course

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.610
of a query in query history.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:57.280
That might be useful for
some of the folks who are

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.910
on our flat-rate pricing model.

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:04.480
And it allows you to calculate
how many slots you really

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:07.000
need to fill your workload.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.850
And finally, on
the bottom there,

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:12.970
one feature that is
really powerful for some

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.820
of the more advanced
users is the ability

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:19.170
to create resource hierarchies.

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:22.350
So you can actually think
of these as virtual clusters

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.260
nested on top of each other.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.050
But they all share resources.

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:28.810
You can have two
separate clusters-- one

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:32.170
for the analytics team, one
for your data science team.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.780
And they all have
perfect isolation.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:37.270
But when your data science
team goes on vacation,

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.070
those resources
are automagically

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:40.660
available for your
analytics team,

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:43.240
so that no resources are wasted.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.820
So here's an example
of a setup like that,

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.810
where we've released
some upgrades

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:50.650
to our monitoring
[INAUDIBLE] that

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:53.110
allow you to track
these hierarchies

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.910
and how they're being consumed.

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.280
So this functionality
is really interesting.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.630
And reach out to us if you
want to learn more about it.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:07.760
Self-service cost controls--
so folks talk to us

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:11.569
and tell us that
sometimes, there's

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:17.089
anxiety with the model of
pricing that BigQuery has,

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:18.829
where you pay for every query.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:21.800
There is anxiety that
you may run a query that

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:22.849
may be too expensive.

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:25.880
Or maybe you'll run
too many queries.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:28.700
Well, we introduced Cost
Controls a couple of years

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:32.360
ago actually to help
you alleviate that pain.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:35.450
Now, what you can do is,
you can set usage limits.

00:17:35.450 --> 00:17:39.120
You can do it per query, or you
can do it as a daily budget.

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:43.520
And this allows you to prevent
the worst-case scenario in case

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:45.650
you really start
spending a lot of money,

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:49.430
and you have really made
a mistake in your code.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.770
This protects you.

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:55.470
The first one is, you can
set a per-query cost limit.

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:58.160
So for example, you
don't want any query

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:00.890
that processes more than
100 terabytes to ever

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:02.180
be ran in the query.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:03.830
Or you can set that limit.

00:18:03.830 --> 00:18:08.250
The second is, you can set
a per-user daily budget.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.610
For example, you
don't want any user

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.970
to run more than 50
terabytes worth of queries

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:15.080
in any single day.

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:17.390
And this, of course,
is a rolling window.

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:20.770
So you don't have to wait
until the end of the day

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:22.010
to have that reset.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:24.810
And finally, you can set
per-project budgets as well.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:28.760
So you can say,
every project only

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:34.290
gets access to 100 terabytes
of processing per day.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:38.291
Now, what happens when you reach
that limit is, BigQuery starts

00:18:38.291 --> 00:18:40.040
giving you an error
that says, hey, you've

00:18:40.040 --> 00:18:42.480
exceeded your limit.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:45.340
You can't run any more
queries until your tokens

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:48.470
are refreshed, until
you start using more.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.520
So what we launched
earlier in May

00:18:52.520 --> 00:18:56.080
is the automation of the
per-project daily budget.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:58.580
So you can go into the
BigQuery console today.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:00.230
You can set the
per-project limit,

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:04.160
and this will take
effect in minutes

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.200
instead of having to go through
support and things like that.

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:11.300
And of course, this is a very
interesting topic of discussion

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:13.180
for our customers,
for our users.

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:16.130
So we'd love to hear
more about what else

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:17.800
you'd like to see
from these features.

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:28.550
Finally, BigQuery is being
used for folks from all parts

00:19:28.550 --> 00:19:30.200
with all kinds of technologies.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:33.170
So people who are used to
relational databases, people

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:36.830
who are used to Hadoop,
maybe they're using Kafka,

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:39.470
would like to use all those
technologies in conjunction

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:40.440
with BigQuery.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:44.840
So we're really focusing
on interoperability.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:48.080
The first feature I want to
talk about is partitions.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:51.090
Historically-- it was six years
ago when we first started--

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:53.360
we didn't have any
types of partitions.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:59.420
We had something
called Table Wildcards.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:07.650
Table Wildcards allow you
to query table data itself.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.160
So if you have 1,000
tables, they're

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:12.830
all named a similar way, you
can use this functionality

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:15.530
to pick out which tables
you really want to query.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.780
So that's been really
powerful for a long time.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:19.970
But throughout the
years, folks have asked

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:22.520
for a partition functionality.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.030
So first, we released
a metadata, what

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.500
we called natural partitions--

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:30.980
partitions on a column
that doesn't exist,

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:35.990
that had a partition
time predicate.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:38.420
But since then, folks
had asked for the ability

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:43.270
to set actual user-based
time-stamped columns

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:44.670
as partition keys.

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.580
So in this example,
you have a table

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:50.750
that has four columns, ts
and the other three columns

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:51.750
there listed.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.920
And we set ts as
the partition key.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.870
So it's data partition key.

00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:59.790
So when you are querying
this table, and you say,

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:03.650
I want ts to be a specific
date time, we will actually

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:06.530
go to just that
partition, hopefully

00:21:06.530 --> 00:21:10.970
as the graph demonstrates.

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:13.550
So this is a really
useful feature.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:16.250
It's also useful when you are
loading data into BigQuery,

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:19.400
or when you are trying to
materialize partition tables

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:22.130
because you can materialize
lots and lots of partitions

00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:25.610
with a single command--

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:29.670
because we will look at the time
stamp of every individual row.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:31.790
And we'll put it in
the right partition.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:35.180
Hopefully, that's
useful for you.

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:39.830
The next feature is Data
Manipulation Language

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:42.300
on partition tables.

00:21:42.300 --> 00:21:44.530
So we've released petitions.

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.610
We've released Data
Manipulation Language.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:48.970
Now, we're kind of merging
these functions together.

00:21:48.970 --> 00:21:54.270
So you can actually edit
partition tables with DML.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:59.510
So in this particular example,
you see that in case I want

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:02.690
to take data from one
partition and do a filter--

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:04.880
where it says
field 1 equals 21--

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:07.390
and move that into a
different partition.

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:10.790
So it's just a very
basic DML statement.

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:14.047
But it allows you to do a lot
with your partition tables.

00:22:14.047 --> 00:22:15.380
Hopefully, you find that useful.

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:22.700
The next feature is
Parquet file format ingest.

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:28.344
So if you read the Dremel paper
that was released in 2010,

00:22:28.344 --> 00:22:30.260
there's a couple of
concepts that are actually

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:31.670
explained in there.

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:34.280
The first one is the Dremel
execution engine, which

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:37.160
is really what powers BigQuery.

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:40.790
And Dremel and BigQuery
are now the same thing.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:43.160
The Dremel team is
now a BigQuery team.

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.062
And they both kind
of go hand in hand.

00:22:45.062 --> 00:22:47.270
But the other concept that
was explained in the paper

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.970
is our file format
called [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:52.100
[INAUDIBLE] is a
common file format

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:58.790
that Julian and other
folks on the Parquet team

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:02.660
saw as useful and
externalized via Parquet.

00:23:02.660 --> 00:23:05.480
But Parquet is now a very
ubiquitous file format.

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:07.580
It's used essentially
by everyone

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:12.200
who's leveraging [? AGFS, ?]
Hadoop, MapReduce, even Spark.

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.550
So Parquet is very powerful.

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:18.560
So what we've enabled
this year is the ability

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:22.680
to ingest Parquet
directly into BigQuery.

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:26.410
it's a very useful feature
for a couple of reasons.

00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:30.380
For one, just like Avro
file format, it's binary.

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:35.780
So you're not going to have
weird collisions between UTF8

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:36.920
and ASCII.

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:40.550
Whatever data you are loading
into BigQuery from Parquet

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.700
is strongly tied.

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:45.560
The second is, Parquet
is highly parallelizable.

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:49.190
So if you load one terabyte
file of Parquet into BigQuery,

00:23:49.190 --> 00:23:51.470
we're going to be able to
split it into as many chunks

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:56.100
as we feel is necessary to
ingest that data into BigQuery

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:57.890
very quickly.

00:23:57.890 --> 00:24:01.790
Third is, Parquet has a
schema inside of the file.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:03.590
So you don't need
to define a schema.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:06.230
Or we don't need
to infer a schema,

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:08.720
so that you can just
take this Parquet file

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:10.710
and load it into
that BigQuery table.

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:12.457
And we will figure
out all the details.

00:24:12.457 --> 00:24:13.790
So it's really, really powerful.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:15.830
We recommend this.

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:18.790
If you have control over which
files are loaded into BigQuery,

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:22.520
Parquet is a great way to go.

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:27.350
And of course, one quick note
on ingest of data into BigQuery.

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:33.380
Ingest is very
compute-intensive operation.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:35.960
We have to read your files.

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:40.610
We have to process them,
encode them into our format,

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:42.650
compress them, load
them, replicate them

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:44.380
all over the place.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:51.020
Typically, with
technologies like BigQuery,

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:54.140
this compute capacity
is the same capacity

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:57.020
that consumes your
queries, so that if you're

00:24:57.020 --> 00:25:00.050
loading lots of data
into your technology,

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.170
your core capacity may suffer.

00:25:02.170 --> 00:25:04.370
With BigQuery, we have
a unique architecture,

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:06.470
where the query
capacity is entirely

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:08.810
separate from the
ingest capacity.

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:11.930
So that no matter how much data
you're loading into BigQuery,

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:13.460
your queries don't suffer.

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:16.126
And of course, we also don't
charge for these loads.

00:25:16.126 --> 00:25:18.000
So it's kind of like
the best of both worlds.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:19.666
We don't charge for
this, and it doesn't

00:25:19.666 --> 00:25:21.500
affect your query capacity.

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:25.259
We feel like our customers
benefit from this greatly.

00:25:25.259 --> 00:25:27.050
And of course, there
are many other formats

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.990
that are being used in
the world out there.

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:32.692
And we're going to be
announcing more soon.

00:25:32.692 --> 00:25:34.400
And please let us know
if you have a file

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:36.690
format that we don't support.

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:38.952
We'd love to see
which ones are popular

00:25:38.952 --> 00:25:40.910
and which ones we should
support in the future.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.530
The last feature out
of the major ones

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:49.190
that I want to talk about
is our continuous updates

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:50.700
to the Simba drivers--

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:54.275
to the ODBC and JDBC drivers
that we work with Simba

00:25:54.275 --> 00:25:56.840
to put together.

00:25:56.840 --> 00:26:00.170
These updates happen rather
regularly these days.

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:03.260
So we always want folks
to use these drivers

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.990
as a gateway to the query.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:08.930
So some of the things
that we've been working on

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:11.810
is things like support
for partitions, support

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:17.570
for DML and DDL, support
for CMEK, Customer-Managed

00:26:17.570 --> 00:26:18.500
Encryption Keys.

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.510
So everything that
BigQuery has, we

00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:22.820
want in the ODBC
and JDBC drivers.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:26.120
So they're continuously being
updated as BigQuery features

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:28.130
are rolling out.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:33.140
We are also working on
making these drivers faster.

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:35.920
So over the past
six months or so,

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:41.010
we've increased performance by
roughly 60% on these drivers.

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:43.344
And we're going to
continue to focus on that.

00:26:43.344 --> 00:26:44.760
And finally, we
want these drivers

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:48.180
to be mature and stable,
so our customers get

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:52.110
the best experience this way.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:55.830
And one final thing that I
didn't really talk about here

00:26:55.830 --> 00:27:00.870
is, there's much more that's
happening inside of BigQuery.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.540
As you can see, you may
look in our release notes,

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:05.280
you may notice that
we've increased

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:07.780
a whole lot of quotas.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:11.430
So for example, the row and
cell size limit, specifically

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:15.600
for CSV and JSON files has
increased from 10 megabytes

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:16.710
to 100 megabytes.

00:27:16.710 --> 00:27:21.450
So it's just easier for you
to load data into BigQuery.

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:24.870
And the maximum number
of partitions per table

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:30.100
has been increased to 4,000,
and so on, and so forth.

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:31.710
The other thing
to point out there

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:35.460
is, we've extended our SQL
functionality to have the Error

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.620
function and the Safe Prefix.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:40.440
So you can handle errors
a little bit better

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.990
inside of your SQL statements.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.710
Now, a quick segue onto quotas.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:48.910
BigQuery is a
multitenant system.

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:52.500
So a lot of the
subsystems are shared.

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:56.400
And just like in any
technology, there

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.770
are anti-patterns in BigQuery.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.380
There are things that
you as a customer

00:28:01.380 --> 00:28:06.960
are allowed to do that may not
work for you if you continue

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.670
to do that through scale.

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:12.400
And that essentially is the
truth with every technology.

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:19.680
So in order for us to work
with you in the best way

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.440
to use BigQuery, we
have these quotas.

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:25.020
So quotas are there
to protect you guys,

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:27.360
and they're there to
protect us as well.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:31.140
That said, if you
have specific use

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:34.800
cases where certain quotas need
to be raised, please come talk

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:35.870
to us.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:40.020
We're always looking for ways
to improve this particular user

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:41.640
experience.

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:45.450
So the quotas are not immunable.

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:46.900
Many of them can be added.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:57.660
So it would be good if you
do end up in situations where

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.530
you're hitting quotas--
come talk to us,

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:01.470
and maybe we'll work on that.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.610
So so far, BigQuery
has, to recap,

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:10.650
released a number of features.

00:29:10.650 --> 00:29:13.110
Of course, we have
lots of engineers,

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:15.370
and we have lots of folks
working on more features.

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:17.260
So there will be
many, many more.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:20.389
And we'll do another
recap just like this.

00:29:20.389 --> 00:29:21.930
We will walk through
all the features

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:23.760
that we're releasing
moving forward.

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:25.320
But so far, these
are the features

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:28.170
we've released in 2018.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:31.620
Again, please do come talk
to us about these and others

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:34.320
that you may need that
we haven't released.

00:29:34.320 --> 00:29:37.720
And we'd love to see
how we can help you.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:47.040
And to wrap up, we,
as I mentioned before,

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:52.020
have our conference in July
24 and 26 in San Francisco.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:53.145
I will be there.

00:29:53.145 --> 00:29:55.980
The vast majority of
the BigQuery leadership

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:58.200
and engineers will be there.

00:29:58.200 --> 00:29:59.670
And we'd love to talk to you.

00:29:59.670 --> 00:30:01.050
So please come find us.

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:02.110
We'll have a booth.

00:30:02.110 --> 00:30:04.050
We'll have sessions.

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:06.860
We'd love to hear
from you-- and great.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.090
Well, stay tuned for a live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:13.590
minute.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.190
Welcome back.

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:55.010
Now, it's time for the
Q&amp;A. We took some questions

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:55.940
from the audience.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:32:01.110
And if you have more questions,
please, again, reach out to us.

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:04.380
The first one is, when are some
of these beta features that

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:08.550
are talked about going to
go GA, General Availability?

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:13.370
Well, let me tell you how
our features get released

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:15.840
into general availability.

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:17.720
It's kind of like
the maturity scale.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:20.060
By the time features hit
General Availability,

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:21.740
they have a support plan.

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:22.400
We have SLAs.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:24.980
We have our set
[INAUDIBLE] beta engineers

00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:26.250
working on these features.

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:27.560
So they are fully supported.

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:29.905
They're locked down, and
we're fully behind it.

00:32:29.905 --> 00:32:33.150
By the time these
features are beta,

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:36.740
they're essentially
locked down as well.

00:32:36.740 --> 00:32:39.530
From beta to GA, we're
working on maturity,

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:43.490
we're working on performance,
scalability, just

00:32:43.490 --> 00:32:47.180
making sure that the
user experience is really

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:48.304
phenomenal there.

00:32:48.304 --> 00:32:49.720
But by the time
features are beta,

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:51.800
they're fairly
mature [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:54.410
And before they go beta,
of course we have alpha.

00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:57.560
And we have Early
Access programs.

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:00.890
And we're always
looking for folks

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:04.650
to try these features out
before they go beta as well.

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:09.290
So it really depends on the
feature from beta to GA.

00:33:09.290 --> 00:33:13.580
It's rare that we have features
that take more than a year

00:33:13.580 --> 00:33:15.080
to go from beta to GA.

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:19.130
But a lot of these take one
or two quarters generally.

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:21.731
But a lot of folks are using
these features and data

00:33:21.731 --> 00:33:23.480
because they're
incredibly useful already.

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:26.390
So I'm not going to stop you.

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:30.530
Next question is, how can I find
out about upcoming features?

00:33:30.530 --> 00:33:34.400
Well, we have our blog posts.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:35.900
We have Twitter.

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:37.620
We have our release notes.

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:42.270
So we try to maintain a
presence around what--

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:45.290
we're trying to update their
customers and our users

00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:47.710
with what's happening.

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:48.800
But we do have a roadmap.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:50.841
And we do have other
features that are coming up.

00:33:50.841 --> 00:33:53.790
So to learn more about that,
reach out to your account folks

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:58.140
or reach out to us directly
to get these updates.

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:02.210
Third question, I need to update
thousands of rows in a table,

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:05.980
but the limit for DML
statement is only 200.

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:06.740
That's correct.

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:11.030
So today, a per-table limit
for how many DML statements

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:13.980
you can execute is 200.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:18.690
But as I mentioned in one
of the earlier slides,

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:23.090
we don't limit how many
rows can be affected

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:24.560
by any of these statements.

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.560
So any of those
200 statements can

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:32.090
execute millions, or billions,
or trillions of mutations.

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:36.469
You can work on the whole table
all at once if you'd like to.

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:38.600
So again, a best
practice for BigQuery

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.659
is to batch these updates,
and deletes, and inserts

00:34:41.659 --> 00:34:45.600
into singular requests.

00:34:45.600 --> 00:34:47.420
So you can update
thousands of rows

00:34:47.420 --> 00:34:49.940
in a single table using
our correlated, updates

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:52.460
using a subquery in
a single statement

00:34:52.460 --> 00:34:53.880
or a couple of statements.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:59.300
But again, if you have specific
use cases or specific places

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:01.420
where you need these
statements [INAUDIBLE],,

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:02.750
please come talk to us.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:06.560
Maybe there's a proper use
case, and we can help you.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:09.090
What partitioning
schema do you recommend?

00:35:09.090 --> 00:35:13.560
So we have really three types
of ways to partition your data.

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:16.880
The first one is, you
can just shard your data.

00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:20.150
What I mean by that is, you can
create a table for every quote

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:21.320
unquote "partition."

00:35:21.320 --> 00:35:25.700
So you can create a table
for every single day

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:26.840
and name it so.

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:31.640
Or you can say games 2018
or 601, games 2018 or 602,

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:35.150
games 2018 or 603.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:38.150
What we have, as I
mentioned, is the ability

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:41.460
to query specific tables.

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:44.210
So you can say, I want
to query all tables

00:35:44.210 --> 00:35:47.900
where the month is July.

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:50.780
And so we will SQL
all those tables,

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:55.790
and we will only select the
tables that fit your predicate.

00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:57.840
So that's one way of doing that.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:01.820
The next way is, using our
natural partitions, which

00:36:01.820 --> 00:36:05.000
is a metadata column
a date-time column,

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:08.870
that isn't a column that
is inside of your table.

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:10.940
But it's a metadata column.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:14.180
And it uses a partition time
product or a partition date

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:15.420
predicate.

00:36:15.420 --> 00:36:17.340
That's also one way
of doing things.

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:19.910
But I think, the most useful
way, at least from what

00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:25.910
our customers tell us, is
this user-specified column

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:26.870
partition.

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:30.440
So you can actually
set up a table,

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:34.610
where one of your columns,
where one of your values,

00:36:34.610 --> 00:36:36.020
is the partition.

00:36:36.020 --> 00:36:40.250
So you can say, this time-stamp
column is my partition.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:42.170
And please use that.

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:43.910
This is really useful
because, again, you

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:47.150
can load data into
BigQuery, into a table,

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:48.960
into multiple
partitions all at once.

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:51.980
Or you can run a select
[? into ?] statement

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:55.640
that writes data to multiple
partitions all at once.

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:01.370
Last question is, how can
